  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">5</span>
    A roadmap to the reading
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p><strong>Preface.</strong> Nietzsche explains what drove him to write this book: the question of the value of our moral values, which he says has preoccupied him since his early twenties. He describes his method as genealogical rather than psychological or philosophical in the usual sense, and he situates himself in opposition to the "English psychologists" (a label he applies to thinkers like Paul Rée who try to explain morality through evolutionary or utilitarian mechanisms). The Preface is short but essential for understanding what kind of project you are in.</p>
<p><strong>First Treatise: "Good and Evil," "Good and Bad."</strong> This is the argument you came for. Nietzsche traces two historically distinct moral vocabularies. The first, which he calls "master morality," originated with aristocratic, ruling classes who defined "good" as a description of themselves: strong, noble, vital, excellent. "Bad" in this vocabulary is simply the opposite: weak, common, lowly. There is no moral condemnation in the word "bad"; it is a social and aesthetic distinction, not a moral judgment.</p>
<p>The second vocabulary, "slave morality," emerged from a very different source: the weak, the oppressed, those unable to overcome the strong by force. Unable to change their situation directly, they changed their valuation of it. They defined "good" as everything the strong were not: humble, meek, suffering, selfless. And they defined "evil" as precisely what the strong were. This revaluation, which Nietzsche calls the "slave revolt in morality," is the historical event he thinks explains the shape of Western moral consciousness.</p>
<p>Read both sections actively. Nietzsche is making a historical claim, a psychological claim, and an evaluative claim at the same time, and distinguishing which is which is part of the intellectual work of this unit.</p>
    </div>
